WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.135 --> 00:00:02.260
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Get Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:05.280
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.325
I am Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:06.325 --> 00:00:07.950
STEPHANIE WONG: And
I'm Stephanie Wong.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.533
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
And in this season,

00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:11.970
we will share some recipes
involving a major networking

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:12.598
ingredient.

00:00:12.598 --> 00:00:14.140
STEPHANIE WONG:
Cloud load balancers.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:14.700
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So, Stephanie,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.960
what are we cooking today?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.000
STEPHANIE WONG: Today
we are making desserts.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.958
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yum.

00:00:18.958 --> 00:00:20.580
Do I get to sample
some at the end?

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.830
I am a great dessert taster--

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.740
tester-- taster.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.500
STEPHANIE WONG: Either way,
sure, if you like dog food.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.500
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: What?

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.958
STEPHANIE WONG: Yep, because
today we're making dog treats.

00:00:31.958 --> 00:00:34.808
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.590
Imagine you're in charge of a
complex website, Beyond Treat--

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.870
your one-stop shop
for vegan dog treats--

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.250
and it's an online hit.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.530
It continues to face
high amounts of traffic,

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.720
and you're not sure
your website backend can

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.223
handle the amount of traffic
from all over the world.

00:00:55.223 --> 00:00:56.890
You know you need to
use load balancers,

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.342
but the options are confusing.

00:00:58.342 --> 00:00:59.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
I totally agree.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.760
Without a recipe, it can be hard
to know exactly how to settle

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.400
on a load balancing architecture
that meets your needs

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.100
and figure out the prerequisites
you need for the best

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.232
performance without making
much of a dent in the wallet.

00:01:12.232 --> 00:01:13.690
STEPHANIE WONG:
Well, to start, you

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:15.370
need to know what
load balancing is

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.710
and why it's so important
to the long-lasting success

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:18.970
of your application.

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.650
Modern high-traffic websites
serve hundreds of thousands,

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.970
if not millions, of concurrent
requests from users or clients

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.650
and return the correct
text, images, video,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.437
or application data all in
a fast, reliable manner.

00:01:30.437 --> 00:01:32.020
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
You've probably all

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.435
experienced visiting your
favorite website only

00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:38.410
for long wait times, connection
timeout errors, or images

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:39.503
and videos buffering.

00:01:39.503 --> 00:01:41.170
STEPHANIE WONG: And,
a lot of the times,

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.670
this is because
the website backend

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.660
is unable to
cost-effectively scale

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:45.832
to meet these high volumes.

00:01:45.832 --> 00:01:48.040
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Of course,
the logical answer here

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.410
is to add more backend servers
to help server traffic.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.310
But the next question becomes,
how do you distribute traffic

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.470
to those backend
servers based on things

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.260
like capacity and health?

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:00.760
STEPHANIE WONG: This is where
load balancing makes a splash.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.800
Load balancing is the process
of distributing traffic

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.500
across your network of servers
to ensure that the system does

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.870
not get overwhelmed
and all requests

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.190
are handled easily
and efficiently.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.860
There are a number
of delicious reasons

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.720
why load balancing is important.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.030
It lets you distribute
load-balanced resources

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:19.540
in a single or multiple regions,
meet your high availability

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.450
requirements, scale your
resources up or down

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.780
with intelligent autoscaling
and use Cloud content delivery

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.455
network for optimal
content delivery.

00:02:27.455 --> 00:02:29.330
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: With
Cloud Load Balancing,

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.210
you can serve
content as close as

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.630
possible to your users on a
system that can respond to over

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.910
one million queries per second.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.550
To decide which load balancer
best suits your implementation,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.990
you need to think about
whether you need global

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:44.980
versus regional load balancing.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.770
Global load balancing
means backend endpoints

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.380
live in multiple regions,
whereas regional load

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.420
balancing means
backend endpoints live

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.070
in a single region.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.460
External versus internal load
balancing-- and what traffic

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.930
types are you going to serve?

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.180
STEPHANIE WONG: They would
use external load balancers

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.240
to distribute traffic coming
from the internet to their GCP

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.190
network and internal
load balancers

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:08.773
to distribute traffic
within their GCP network.

00:03:08.773 --> 00:03:10.690
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: The
external load balancing

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.660
includes four options--

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:15.790
the HTTP(S) load balancing,
for a HTTP or HTTP(S)

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:19.030
traffic; the TCP
proxy for TCP traffic,

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.380
for ports other than 80 and
8080 without SSL offload;

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.630
the SSL proxy, for SSL offload
on ports other than 80 or 8080;

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.680
or the network load balancing,
for TCP or UDP traffic.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.930
STEPHANIE WONG: While the
global HTTP(S) load balancer

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.960
is for layer 7 traffic and is
built using the Google frontend

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.270
engines at the edge
of Google's network,

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.830
the regional network
load balancer

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:44.970
is for the layer 4 traffic
and is built using Maglevs.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.580
Google built Maglevs in 2008 to
load-balance all traffic that

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:51.010
comes into our data centers
and distribute traffic

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.410
to the frontend engines
at our network edges.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:54.940
The traffic is
distributed to a set

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:56.385
of regional backend instances.

00:03:56.385 --> 00:03:58.510
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I'm not
going to lie, Stephanie,

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:02.030
Maglevs sound like
delicious baked goods.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.155
What makes them so special?

00:04:03.155 --> 00:04:05.488
STEPHANIE WONG: Maglev was a
break from traditional load

00:04:05.488 --> 00:04:08.110
balancers in that it is
software-based and operates

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.870
in an active-active
scale-out architecture.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.580
Maglev evenly
distributes traffic

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.730
over hundreds of backends and
minimizes the negative impact

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.250
of unexpected faults on
connection-oriented protocols.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.700
It's great for lightweight
L4-based load balancing, where

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.250
you want to preserve
the client IP address,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:25.930
all the way to the
backend instance,

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.990
and perform TLS termination
on these instances.

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:31.180
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: As for
HTTP(S) load balancers,

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:33.880
I mentioned that Google
pushed load balancing out

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:36.250
to the edge network
on frontend service,

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.520
as opposed to using
traditional DNS-based approach.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.680
So global load
balancing capacity

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.080
can be behind a single anycast
virtual IPv4 or IPv6 addresses.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.410
This means you can deploy
capacity in multiple regions,

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.140
without having to
modify the DNS entries

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.150
or add new load balancer IP
addresses for new regions.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:00.890
So it's clear that with
global HTTP(S) load balancing,

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:03.943
you get cross-regional
failover and overflow.

00:05:03.943 --> 00:05:05.860
STEPHANIE WONG: And the
distribution algorithm

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.620
automatically directs traffic
to the next closest instance

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.620
with available capacity in the
event of a failure of or lack

00:05:11.620 --> 00:05:14.260
of capacity, for instances in
the region closest to the end

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.040
user.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:17.450
We'll cover this more in
the next couple of episodes.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.900
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: GCP also
offers proxy-based load

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.810
balancers for TCP
and SSL traffic.

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.620
And they use the same globally
distributed infrastructure.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:29.050
Generally speaking, your
decision to use them

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.950
would depend on whether you
require SSL offload or not.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:35.390
You can find out more
in the links below.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:37.750
Now let's get back
to Beyond Treat.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.210
In Beyond Treat's
case, we are only

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.970
going to receive HTTP
and HTTP(S) traffic,

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.920
so they will choose
HTTP(S) load balancer.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.590
But, like most
companies, the website

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.280
also has private workloads,
such as application servers

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.160
that need to be protected
from the public internet.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.650
Those services need
to scale and grow

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.010
behind a private watchful
IP that is accessible only

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:01.780
by internal instances.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:03.700
STEPHANIE WONG: For
this, their best option

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.560
is the regional
layer 7 internal load

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.990
balancing based on
Google's Andromeda network

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.520
virtualization stack.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.400
Similar to the HTTP(S) load
balancer and the network load

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:15.010
balancer, internal
layer 7 load balancing

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.670
is neither a hardware appliance
nor an instance-based solution

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:21.610
and can support as many
connections per second

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:23.320
as you need, since
there is no load

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.120
balancer in the path
between your client

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.557
and the backend instances.

00:06:26.557 --> 00:06:28.390
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Over
the next few videos,

00:06:28.390 --> 00:06:30.730
we'll follow Beyond
Treats as they grow,

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:32.830
and even experience
unpredictable spikes

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:35.860
in traffic, like when it's
International Dog Day and Big

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.560
Bone treat orders
are up the wazoo.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.290
This makes their website
an ideal candidate

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.840
for explaining all the
load balancing options

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:45.172
you have at your disposal.

00:06:45.172 --> 00:06:46.630
STEPHANIE WONG:
But, of course, you

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.000
can apply these principles
described in these videos

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.718
to a wide range of workloads.

00:06:50.718 --> 00:06:52.510
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, there you have it.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:54.427
STEPHANIE WONG: Load
balancing deconstructed--

00:06:54.427 --> 00:06:56.062
my dog is going
to be so excited,

00:06:56.062 --> 00:06:58.020
and he might even make
an appearance next time.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:58.990
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Wow.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.060
That's all for today on
Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.790
We are hoping that you are
making the correct load

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.968
balancing choice for
your architecture.

00:07:05.968 --> 00:07:07.760
If you would like to
see more such content,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.843
don't forget to like and
subscribe to our channel.

00:07:09.843 --> 00:07:13.920
